      "summary": "backgrounds/grants.go (172 lines) and races/grants.go (109 lines) have no test files",
      "pointer": "rpg-toolkit#615",
      "added": "2026-05-02",
      "status": "open"
    },
    {
      "id": "disc-005",
      "summary": "LayoutOrchestrator and TransitionSystem interfaces defined in orchestrator.go with no implementations and no 'not implemented' marker",
      "details": "tools/spatial/orchestrator.go:109 defines LayoutOrchestrator (ArrangeRooms, CalculateRoomPositions, ValidateLayout, GetLayoutMetrics). orchestrator.go:135 defines TransitionSystem (BeginTransition, CompleteTransition, CancelTransition, GetActiveTransitions, GetTransition). Both have zero implementations. tools/spatial/CLAUDE.md documents them as 'future work' but the source file has no // Not implemented comment. A new contributor cannot distinguish implemented interfaces from advisory ones by reading the source. Risk: someone implements against a contract that is actually advisory.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "disc-006",
      "summary": "mechanics/features base module has zero test files — loader and SimpleFeature are untested",
      "details": "ls mechanics/features/ shows: README.md, doc.go, docs/, errors.go, feature.go, go.mod, go.sum, loader.go, mock/, simple_feature.go. No *_test.go files. Running go test ./... from mechanics/features returns 'no test files' — not a failure, but loader.Route() error paths and simple_feature.go behaviors are untested at the module level. Indirect coverage exists via rulebooks/dnd5e/features tests.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "disc-007",
      "summary": "rulebooks/dnd5e/dungeon/ is inside the rulebook but architecturally belongs at Tools layer",
      "details": "rulebooks/dnd5e/dungeon/ (dungeon.go, dungeon_data.go, types.go, dungeon_test.go) provides procedural dungeon generation. It depends on tools/environments and tools/spatial (correct direction — lower layers). But its location means rpg-api must import the full dnd5e rulebook to use dungeon logic. The planned move is to tools/dungeon/ or a new top-level module. No issue filed as of 2026-05-02. dungeon_test.go is 336 lines — solid test coverage, no bugs known, just wrong location.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "disc-008",
      "summary": "character/choices/testdata/ provenance undocumented — fetched from an external API, no refresh instructions",
      "details": "rulebooks/dnd5e/character/choices/testdata/api/classes/ and testdata/api/races/ contain JSON fixtures. No file documents when they were fetched, from which URL, or how to refresh them. If the upstream API changes its schema, tests silently test stale data. Low priority but worth noting before a new contributor modifies the choice system.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "disc-009",
      "summary": "spawn module's Phase 2 patterns (formation, team, clustered) have no standalone tests",
      "details": "tools/spawn/spawning_patterns.go implements formation, team-based, player choice, and clustered spawning. No test file exercises these patterns directly. Tested only through the engine integration test. A bug in formation logic would not be caught by cd tools/spawn && go test ./...",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "disc-010",
      "summary": "graph_generator.go in tools/environments is substantial (estimated 400 lines) with no direct unit tests",
      "details": "tools/environments/graph_generator.go generates connected room graphs with configurable parameters. No direct test file exists for this file. It is tested indirectly through BasicEnvironment creation tests in environment_persistence_test.go. A procedural generation algorithm with no unit tests is a gap — edge cases (degenerate inputs, zero rooms, disconnected graph) are not explicitly verified.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "disc-011",
      "summary": "The dual-bus pattern (EventBus vs BusEffect) has no ADR and is easy to misuse",
      "details": "events/bus.go defines EventBus (plain pub/sub). events/bus_effect.go defines BusEffect (Apply/Remove lifecycle). These are distinct concepts. ADR-0024 covers typed topics but does not explain when to use EventBus directly vs. routing through BusEffect. The correct heuristic: EventBus for stateless observers; BusEffect for stateful mechanics with an active/inactive lifecycle. Without documentation, new contributors wire the wrong one.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    }
  ]
}

text:
{
  "items": [
    {
      "id": "lesson-001",
      "summary": "EntityType is a named type, not a raw string — mocks must match the exact return type",
      "details": "core.EntityType is defined as 'type EntityType string' — a distinct named type. A mock that returns string instead of EntityType compiles if the mock does not claim to satisfy an interface, but fails when assigned to core.Entity. The items/validation test failure (issue #612) is the canonical example. Always check: if an interface method returns a named type, your mock must return that exact named type.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-002",
      "summary": "Never commit replace directives — they are dev-only conveniences that break CI",
      "details": "Local replace directives (replace github.com/foo => ../foo) resolve against local filesystem paths. CI does not have those paths. Even when CI checks out the full repo, go mod tidy produces a diff from the replace directives, which CI can treat as a failure. The CLAUDE.md rule is unambiguous: NEVER add replace directives. Remove them before committing. The four modules in issue #613 are the result of development shortcuts that became permanent.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-003",
      "summary": "Bulk error collection in Remove() — PR #603",
      "details": "BusEffect.Remove() must unsubscribe all active handlers, not stop at the first failure. PR #603 fixed Condition.Remove() to collect all unsubscribe errors. The pattern: var errs []error; for each ID, if unsubscribe fails append to errs; set subscriptions nil; return errors.Join(errs...). Stopping at first failure leaves orphaned subscriptions that fire on events after removal.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-004",
      "summary": "make pre-commit only covers core + events — not the full repo",
      "details": "The Makefile pre-commit target runs fmt, tidy, lint, and tests for core and events only. Before committing changes to mechanics/*, tools/*, rulebooks/*, items run per-module go test ./... and golangci-lint run ./... manually. make test-all covers all modules but currently fails at items/ (issue #612).",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-005",
      "summary": "rulebooks/dnd5e/dungeon has correct dependency direction but wrong layer location",
      "details": "dungeon/ imports tools/environments and tools/spatial (lower layers — correct). The problem is not a dependency inversion but the module location: dungeon logic belongs at the Tools layer so rpg-api can import dungeon generation without importing the full dnd5e rulebook. The planned move is architectural relocation, not a dependency fix.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-006",
      "summary": "Toolkit ADR and journey docs are load-bearing context, not archive material — do not apply platform-mcp doc shape mechanically",
      "details": "The previous invocation bootstrapped docs by adding copies of docs/adr/ and docs/journey/ into docs/archive/, treating them as legacy. Kirk corrected: the ADRs and journey docs record how the engine came to be and are top-level docs, not archive material. The originals were never moved (they stayed at docs/adr/ and docs/journey/ all along); the archive copies were pure duplication added by the bootstrap commit. Fix: git rm the 116 archive copies. Rule going forward: ADRs stay forever (superseded ones get a note, not deletion); journey docs stay as exploration history. The platform-mcp doc shape is a starting point, not a constraint — toolkit is foundational in a way other repos aren't. When in doubt, the toolkit's own history matters more than structural uniformity.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-007",
      "summary": "Plans judgment call: toolkit docs/plans/ is top-level; the single legitimately-archived plan stays in docs/archive/plans/",
      "details": "docs/plans/ contains 10 feature design plans (Dec 2025-Jan 2026). These are toolkit-specific exploration — some implemented, some stale — and they live at top-level because they document the toolkit's design history. A single older plan (2025-12-06-ac-system-design.md) was legitimately moved into docs/archive/plans/ by commit 3dd2b98 (Dec 2025) before the ideas/plans distinction was clear; it has no duplicate in docs/plans/ and was left in place. The 10 copies added by the bootstrap commit were removed.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    },
    {
      "id": "lesson-008",
      "summary": "Ideas judgment call: toolkit docs/ideas/ is top-level; it is toolkit-specific design memory, not rpg-project/ideas/ territory",
      "details": "docs/ideas/ has 3 idea directories (action-economy-history, monster-behavior, type-safe-refs). These are toolkit-scoped design explorations that predate the rpg-project/ideas/ convention. rpg-project/ideas/ owns cross-repo ideas; toolkit/docs/ideas/ owns toolkit-internal design history. They are not duplicates. The archive copies were removed; the originals stay at top-level.",
      "added": "2026-05-02"
    }
  ]
}

